+# 0001: Plan changes: upgrades are prorated now, everything else waits for period end
+
+- Status: accepted
+- Date: 2026-10-29
+- Spec: `specs/plan-changes.md`
+
+## Context
+
+Customers need to change plan mid-cycle without support canceling and resubscribing them.
+Nobody may pay twice for the same days. The package has no credit balance and no refunds.
+Invoices issued under this policy are permanent records, so the policy is hard to change
+later.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A change counts as an upgrade or a downgrade by comparing the plans' `monthly_price` only,
+  whatever interval the customer is on or moving to.
+- **Upgrades apply immediately.** One invoice is issued today with a credit for the current
+  plan and a charge for the new plan over the remaining days of the current period. Both lines
+  use the current interval's prices.
+  - Each line is `price × remaining_days / period_days`, computed exactly and rounded half up
+    to cents on its own. The total is the sum of the rounded lines.
+  - The period dates don't change.
+- **Downgrades, same-price (lateral) changes and every interval switch are scheduled** for the
+  end of the current period. Nothing is billed at the time of the request. The renewal then
+  bills the scheduled plan and interval at full price.
+  - We never refund unused annual time, and we never start a new period mid-cycle.
+- An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split: the upgrade applies now, and the
+  interval switch is scheduled.
+- A subscription holds at most one scheduled change.
+  - Each request replaces it.
+  - An upgrade without an interval switch discards it.
+  - Requesting the current plan and interval cancels it.
+  - Canceling the subscription discards it.
+  - A scheduled change applies even if its plan is retired before the renewal.
+- During a free trial, every change applies immediately and bills nothing. The trial end
+  doesn't move.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No credit balance is needed. A change invoice can only total below zero if a plan with a
+  higher monthly price has a lower annual price; the current catalogue never prices plans
+  like that.
+- Customers who downgrade keep the plan they paid for until the end of the period.
+- Future work (e.g. immediate downgrades with credits, or changes on chosen dates) has to
+  supersede this ADR, because existing invoices were computed under it.
