+# Mid-cycle plan changes
+
+## What and why
+
+Customers can't change plan today: support has to cancel and resubscribe them by hand.
+This adds self-service plan changes to the billing package: upgrades and downgrades,
+optionally combined with a switch between monthly and annual billing. They are prorated so
+nobody pays twice for the same days, and we never refund unused time.
+
+## Interface (fixed by the requester, verbatim)
+
+- `BillingService.change_plan(subscription_id: str, new_plan_code: str, new_interval: Interval | None = None) -> Invoice | None`
+  in `src/billing/service.py`. `new_interval=None` keeps the subscription's current interval.
+- Return the invoice issued for the change (recorded in the store like every other invoice),
+  or `None` when nothing is billed at the moment of the request.
+- Add `InvalidPlanChange(BillingError)` to `src/billing/errors.py` for change requests that
+  can't be honoured; keep using the existing error classes where they already fit.
+
+## Rules
+
+- **Target.** Each request states the full target: the plan, plus the interval (the current
+  one when `new_interval` is `None`). It replaces whatever change was scheduled before.
+- **Classification.** It depends only on the plans' `monthly_price`, whatever interval the
+  customer is on or moving to:
+  - a more expensive plan is an **upgrade**;
+  - a cheaper plan is a **downgrade**;
+  - a different plan at the same monthly price is a **lateral change**;
+  - the same plan with another interval is an **interval switch** only.
+- **When changes take effect.**
+  - An upgrade takes effect immediately.
+  - Downgrades, lateral changes and every interval switch (in either direction) are
+    **scheduled** for the end of the current period. A downgrade or lateral change combined
+    with an interval switch is scheduled as a whole.
+  - An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split: the upgrade happens now, and the
+    interval switch is scheduled for the end of the period.
+- **Proration of an upgrade.**
+  - `remaining_days = period_end − today`.
+  - `period_days = period_end − period_start` (the actual length of the current period).
+  - The invoice is issued today and has exactly two lines:
+    - a credit of `−(current plan price × remaining_days / period_days)`;
+    - a charge of `+(new plan price × remaining_days / period_days)`.
+  - Both lines use the prices for the **current** interval.
+  - Each line is computed exactly and rounded to cents (half up) on its own; there is no
+    rounded daily rate. The invoice total is the sum of the two rounded lines.
+- **Scheduled changes.** A scheduled change hasn't happened yet. An upgrade is prorated from
+  the plan the customer is on now, never from the scheduled one. At the end of the period,
+  the renewal starts a period of the scheduled plan and interval and bills that plan's full
+  price for that interval.
+- **Trials.** During a free trial, any change (upgrade, downgrade, lateral or interval
+  switch) applies immediately. It bills nothing and doesn't move the trial end. When the
+  trial ends, the full price of the plan and interval the customer is on by then is billed.
+- **Rejections.** A rejected request changes nothing: no invoice is issued and the
+  subscription is left untouched.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+Prices used below: Basic 10/100, Pro 30/300, Premium 75/750 (monthly/annual); Team is 30/280.
+
+1. On Pro monthly for 2026-03-01 → 2026-04-01, `change_plan(sub, "premium")` on 2026-03-11:
+   - returns an invoice issued 2026-03-11 with lines −20.32 and +50.81, total 30.49;
+   - the invoice is recorded in the store;
+   - the subscription is now on Premium with the same period dates.
+2. After the upgrade in (1), the renewal on 2026-04-01 bills Premium's full monthly price,
+   75.00.
+3. An upgrade on an annual subscription is prorated with annual prices. Basic annual,
+   2026-01-15 → 2027-01-15, upgraded to Pro on 2026-07-15 (184 of 365 days remaining):
+   lines −50.41 and +151.23.
+4. A downgrade returns `None` and issues no invoice. The customer stays on the current plan
+   until `period_end`. The renewal then bills the cheaper plan's full price.
+5. An interval switch on the same plan, in either direction, returns `None` and issues no
+   invoice. At `period_end` a period of the new interval starts, and that interval's full
+   price is billed (e.g. monthly → annual: the next period is 12 months long and billed at
+   the annual price).
+6. A downgrade combined with an interval switch returns `None`. At `period_end` both take
+   effect together.
+7. An upgrade combined with an interval switch:
+   - returns an invoice now, prorated at the current interval's prices;
+   - the subscription is on the new plan immediately, still on its current interval;
+   - at `period_end` the new interval starts and its full price is billed.
+8. `new_interval=None` keeps the current interval: no interval switch is scheduled.
+9. A new request replaces the scheduled change. For example, Basic is scheduled and then
+   Pro annual is requested on a Premium monthly subscription; the renewal bills Pro annual.
+10. An upgrade discards any scheduled change unless the same request schedules an interval
+    switch. Example: on Pro monthly 2026-03-01 → 2026-04-01 with Basic scheduled, requesting
+    Premium on 2026-03-11 returns an invoice totalling 30.49. The renewal bills Premium
+    monthly, 75.00.
+11. Requesting the current plan and interval while a change is scheduled cancels the scheduled
+    change. It returns `None` and bills nothing; the renewal bills the current plan.
+12. Requesting the current plan and interval with nothing scheduled raises
+    `InvalidPlanChange`.
+13. A lateral change (different plan, same monthly price) is scheduled: it returns `None`
+    and issues no invoice. The renewal bills the new plan.
+14. During a trial, an upgrade, downgrade or interval switch:
+    - returns `None` and issues no invoice;
+    - changes the plan and interval immediately;
+    - leaves the trial end unchanged.
+
+    At trial end, the full price of the plan and interval the customer is on then is billed.
+15. Errors:
+    - an unknown subscription raises `SubscriptionNotFound`;
+    - an unknown plan raises `PlanNotFound`;
+    - a canceled subscription raises `SubscriptionNotActive`;
+    - moving to a retired plan other than the current one raises `PlanNotAvailable`;
+    - a request on or after `period_end`, before `renew_due` has run, raises
+      `InvalidPlanChange`.
+16. Every rejected request leaves the subscription unchanged and issues no invoice.
+17. Retired plans:
+    - a customer on a retired plan can move off it;
+    - an interval switch on the retired plan they're on is allowed (and scheduled);
+    - a downgrade scheduled to a plan that is retired before the renewal still applies at
+      the renewal.
+18. Canceling a subscription discards its scheduled change.
+19. `InvalidPlanChange` is a subclass of `BillingError` and is exported from `billing`.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Refunds, credit balances, or invoices that pay money back for unused time.
+- Immediate interval switches, or starting a new period on the day of a change.
+- Changes effective on a chosen future date other than the end of the current period.
+- Proration of trial time; payment collection; taxes; discounts; notifications.
+
+## Scope limits
+
+- Touches `billing.service`, `billing.subscriptions` (the subscription carries its scheduled
+  change), `billing.errors`, the package exports, the README, and tests.
+- No new dependencies. Existing behaviour of `subscribe`, `renew_due` and `cancel` is
+  unchanged for subscriptions without a scheduled change.
